Yes. If you strip away the theology for a moment and look at the communication pattern, a lot of Jesus’s teaching can be read as frame transformation rather than simple argument.

He often does not fight inside the frame he is handed. He changes the frame.

Someone asks for a binary judgment, and he answers with a question. Someone defines status one way, and he inverts the hierarchy. Someone asks about the letter of a rule, and he shifts attention to purpose, mercy, intent, or consequence. Someone tries to force a local tactical choice, and he moves to a larger temporal or moral scale.

The reusable pattern is something like:

\text{received frame}
\rightarrow
\text{identify hidden assumption}
\rightarrow
\text{shift scale / category / perspective}
\rightarrow
\text{restate governing principle}
\rightarrow
\text{return to action}

That is different from mere persuasion. It is more like changing the coordinate system in which the problem is being interpreted.

You could break the pattern into operators such as:

* Question instead of answer — forces the other person to expose assumptions.
* Parable — moves the problem into a safer symbolic space where the structure becomes visible.
* Scale shift — moves from immediate dispute to larger principle.
* Role inversion — servant/master, first/last, strong/weak.
* Temporal reframing — present suffering versus longer-horizon meaning.
* Identity reframing — treating a person as more than the category imposed on them.
* Refusal of adversarial framing — not accepting the opponent’s forced binary.
* Embodied demonstration — showing the pattern through action rather than merely describing it.

Then your contribution is the explicit mechanics:

\text{example}
\rightarrow
\text{extract operator}
\rightarrow
\text{test across contexts}
\rightarrow
\text{identify failure modes}
\rightarrow
\text{train until intuitive}

That last step connects directly to what you were just saying about reflex. Eventually you do not need to think, “Now I will perform a frame inversion.” You recognize the trap and naturally step outside it.

The ethical dividing line is important, though: frame control used on yourself or transparently in dialogue is different from covertly engineering another person’s perception for your own advantage. The stronger form is not “make them see what I want.” It is “make the hidden frame visible enough that everyone can choose more deliberately.”

That makes it less like manipulation and more like frame literacy: recognizing the battlefield, seeing who chose its boundaries, and knowing when to remain inside them, redraw them, or simply refuse the game.